<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athanasius of Alexandria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 296–373 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Athanasius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athanasius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was patriarch of Alexandria in the fourth century and a leading defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a>. He consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frumentius">Frumentius</a> as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>. This established the link between the Ethiopian church and the Church of Alexandria, a significant aspect of religious governance in the Aksumite Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="Athanasius of Alexandria" width="291" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Byzantine Institute staff · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fethiye_Camii,_parekklesion,_diakonikon,_mosaics,_Istanbul,_Turkey_-_South_wall,_St._Athanasius,_detail_of_upper_half_-_MSBZ004_BF_T_F_027_B_-_Dumbarton_Oaks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Athanasius was born into a Christian family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, or possibly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> town of Damanhur, between 293 and 298. His parents were wealthy enough to give him a fine secular education, though he was clearly not a member of the Egyptian aristocracy. He wrote most of his surviving works in Greek, but he was also fluent in Coptic, and some surviving copies of his writings are in Coptic. Rufinus tells a story that Bishop Alexander saw boys imitating baptism on the seashore, with Athanasius acting as bishop, and invited them to prepare for clerical careers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>About 319 the presbyter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, whose subordinationist teaching held that Christ the Logos was made and not begotten, came into conflict with Alexander and was excommunicated. He had the support of the powerful bishop Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>. In 325 Athanasius, then a deacon and about 27 years old, took a leading part against the Arians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> as Alexander's assistant. Alexander recommended him as successor on his deathbed, and according to the accounts Athanasius was elected by the bishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> amid the crowd's cries for him, probably in 326 or 328, his enemies later alleging that he was under the canonical age of 35.</p><p>His episcopate lasted 45 years, with interruptions. He faced repeated conflict with the Arians and with the emperors Constantine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>, Julian the Apostate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>, which brought five exiles totalling 17 years. His critics accused him of defiling an altar and of selling grain meant for the poor, and scholars note that he used the label Arian for many who did not consider themselves followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>. He died on 2 May 373.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Athanasius was not a speculative theologian and held to the tradition proclaimed by the apostles and guarded by the fathers. He held that the Son and the Holy Spirit are consubstantial with the Father, which strongly influenced later doctrine of the Trinity. His two early treatises, Against the Heathen and On the Incarnation, were probably written about 318, before the Arian controversy. In the second he argued that the Word, through whom God created the world, entered it in human form to lead people back to the harmony from which they had fallen.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/life-of-antony">Life of Antony</a>, a biography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-the-great">Anthony the Great</a>, became his most widely read work and helped spread the ascetic ideal in East and West. His 39th Festal Letter, written in 367, is widely regarded as a milestone in the development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> canon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Athanasius of Alexandria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The adoption of Christianity under Ezana in the mid-fourth century, following the influence of his Syrian-Greek tutor Frumentius, who was consecrated as the first Bishop of Aksum by <b>Athanasius of Alexandria</b>, made the church a main support of government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Athanasius</b>, bishop of Alexandria from 328 to 373, defended the creed of the Council of Nicaea against the followers of Arius, an Alexandrian priest, and his Life of Antony carried the monastic ideal west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its contents were broadly agreed by the 4th century; a letter of <b>Athanasius of Alexandria</b> in 367 is the first to list exactly the twenty-seven books.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=346" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Athanasius of Alexandria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=346">What was here in 346 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
